" (https://arxiv.org/pdf/1212.4588)\nciteturn15view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 9; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1212.4588\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1471\nL0@P0: Virtual potentials for feedback traps\nL1@P0: Yonggun Jun and John Bechhoefer\nL2@P0: Department of Physics, Simon Fraser University, Burnaby, B.C., V5A 1S6, Canada\nL3@P0: (Dated: July 1, 2021)\nL4@P0: The recently developed feedback trap can be used to create arbitrary virtual potentials, to explore\nL5@P0: the dynamics of small particles or large molecules in complex situations. Experimentally, feedback\nL6@P0: traps introduce several finite time scales: there is a delay between the measurement of a particle’s\nL7@P0: position and the feedback response; the feedback response is applied for a finite update time; and a\nL8@P0: finite camera exposure integrates motion. We show how to incorporate such timing effects into the\nL9@P0: description of particle motion. For the test case of a virtual quadratic potential, we give the first\nL10@P0: accurate description of particle dynamics, calculating the power spectrum and variance of fluctuations\nL11@P0: as a function of feedback gain, testing against simulations. We show that for small feedback gains, the\nL12@P0: motion approximates that of a particle in an ordinary harmonic potential. Moreover, if the potential\nL13@P0: is varied in time, for example by varying its stiffness, the work that is calculated approximates that\nL14@P0: done in an ordinary changing potential. The quality of the approximation is set by the ratio of the\nL15@P0: update time of the feedback loop to the relaxation time of motion in the virtual potential.\nL16@P0: PACS numbers: 05.40.-a, 87.19.lr, 87.15.Vv\nL17@P0: I. INTRODUCTION\nL18@P0: In 2005, Cohen and Moerner introduced the Anti\u0002Brownian ELectrokinetic (ABEL) trap, a new experimen\u0002tal technique for studying long-time dynamical properties\nL19@P0: of small particles and molecules. One key advantage over\nL20@P0: other trapping techniques such as optical or magnetic\nL21@P0: tweezers is its ability to trap molecules and sub-micron\nL22@P0: particles directly rather than via the micron-sized particles\nL23@P0: of the former techniques. The ABEL trap uses feedback to\nL24@P0: counteract the random thermal fluctuations that perturb\nL25@P0: the motion of small objects in a finite-temperature fluid\nL26@P0: [1], with electrokinetic forces being one way among many\nL27@P0: to apply restoring forces; it is thus perhaps more simply\nL28@P0: termed a feedback trap. The basic idea is to observe the\nL29@P0: position of an object, compare its estimated position with\nL30@P0: a desired position, and, as rapidly as possible, apply a\nL31@P0: corrective force to move the particle towards the desired\nL32@P0: position. To trap small objects, the observations must be\nL33@P0: rapid, as the particle diffuses “out of control” during the\nL34@P0: time ts between corrections. Indeed, the lower limit to\nL35@P0: the size of particle that can be trapped depends directly\nL36@P0: on ts. Recently, Fields and Cohen, by responding to every\nL37@P0: detected photon, were able to trap for several seconds a\nL38@P0: single fluorescent dye molecule diffusing in water [2].\nL39@P0: Cohen has also shown that a feedback trap can do\nL40@P0: more than just trap a particle: it is also possible to place\nL41@P0: the particle in an arbitrary “virtual” potential [3]. The\nL42@P0: protocol to approximate motion in a potential U(x) works\nL43@P0: as follows: Let the estimated position of the particle at\nL44@P0: time step n, in units of a sampling period ts, be x¯n. (We\nL45@P0: distinguish between the observed position x¯n and the true\nL46@P0: position xn.) Then, at time step n, we apply the force\nL47@P0: Fn = −∂xU(x¯n), held constant over the interval ts. In\nL48@P0: [3], experimental evidence is given that the probability\nL49@P0: distribution of observed positions x¯ obeys the Boltzmann\nL50@P0: distribution ρ(¯x) ∝ exp[−U(¯x)/kBT].\nL51@P0: In the above scheme, the potential imposed is a virtual\nL52@P0: one that is imposed by the rules of the feedback loop.\nL53@P0: Take away the feedback loop, and there is only a particle\nL54@P0: diffusing in a fluid. But then it is fair to ask, In what sense\nL55@P0: is the motion of a discrete closed-loop feedback system\nL56@P0: equivalent to a “real” potential? After all, the dynamics\nL57@P0: is discretized at a time scale ts. Reasoning by analogy to\nL58@P0: computer simulations of the Langevin equation, we expect\nL59@P0: that as ts → 0, the closed-loop feedback system will be\nL60@P0: equivalent to the desired continuous dynamical system.\nL61@P0: But experiments are always done at finite ts, and the in\u0002formation is acted upon only after a delay, td. In addition,\nL62@P0: the position is typically measured (with random error)\nL63@P0: by integrating a camera over a finite time tc that can be\nL64@P0: comparable to ts. Under such conditions, is the motion\nL65@P0: truly equivalent to the desired potential? Are there correc\u0002tions to the “naive potential”? If so, are such corrections\nL66@P0: important in a given application? Likewise, can one use\nL67@P0: virtual potentials for thermodynamic calculations such as\nL68@P0: the work done by a changing potential?\nL69@P0: We will find that the answers to these questions are\nL70@P0: mathematically boring—the situation resembles that of a\nL71@P0: continuous system with discrete observations—but physi\u0002cally exciting: we can now study the motion of a particle\nL72@P0: in an arbitrary potential or force field and learn about\nL73@P0: both dynamic and thermodynamic quantities.\nL74@P0: Indeed, this article was motivated by attempts to use a\nL75@P0: feedback trap to explore Landauer’s Principle that erasing\nL76@P0: information in a memory element requires a finite amount\nL77@P0: of work [4, 5]. After making preliminary measurements\nL78@P0: reporting a qualitative observation of the effect [6], we\nL79@P0: realized that there were systematic deviations from calcu\u0002lations based on a continuous potential that needed to be\nL80@P0: understood before a quantitative study could be made.\nL81@P0: The calculations reported below address these concerns.\nL82@P0-1: arXiv:1212.4588v1 [cond-mat.stat-mech] 19 Dec 20122\nL83@P1: II. DYNAMICS OF A PARTICLE IN A\nL84@P1: QUADRATIC VIRTUAL POTENTIAL\nL85@P1: In this section, we explore the dynamics of a feedback\nL86@P1: trap with an imposed quadratic virtual potential. The\nL87@P1: goal will be to calculate the power spectrum and variance\nL88@P1: of the particle’s position fluctuations. The calculation is\nL89@P1: complicated by the presence of three short time scales,\nL90@P1: which are comparable but not in general equal:\nL91@P1: 1. The output is updated after an observation with a\nL92@P1: delay time td .\nL93@P1: 2. The observation is made via a camera exposure of\nL94@P1: duration tc.\nL95@P1: 3. The update is applied for a time ts, which is also\nL96@P1: the periodicity of camera exposures (one exposure\nL97@P1: starts every ts, which may be longer than tc).\nL98@P1: As we will see, previous work has not described all the\nL99@P1: consequences of these experimental complications. Below,\nL100@P1: we will see how to incorporate them by solving a series of\nL101@P1: increasingly complicated problems:\nL102@P1: 1. The diffusion dynamics of a Brownian particle with\nL103@P1: observations every ts that are integrated over tc,\nL104@P1: with a response that is delayed by a time td = ts;\nL105@P1: 2. feedback trapping in a virtual quadratic potential,\nL106@P1: with noiseless, instantaneous position measurements,\nL107@P1: whose results are available with no delay;\nL108@P1: 3. feedback trapping adding a camera exposure tc and\nL109@P1: a delay td = ts;\nL110@P1: 4. feedback trapping in the general case where td 6= ts.\nL111@P1: A. Free diffusion\nL112@P1: We begin by considering the one-dimensional free diffu\u0002sion of a Brownian particle that is observed via camera\nL113@P1: exposures that measure the average position over the\nL114@P1: exposure time tc. There is one exposure every ts inter\u0002val, and the mid-point of the exposure time is delayed\nL115@P1: by one time step, td = ts. We distinguish between the\nL116@P1: actual position of a particle at time nts, denoted by xn\nL117@P1: and the corresponding observation of that position (as\nL118@P1: deduced from the camera exposure), denoted x¯n. The\nL119@P1: above statements are illustrated in the timing diagram\nL120@P1: shown in Fig. 1.\nL121@P1: We start with the equation of motion of the Brownian\nL122@P1: particle, assuming complete overdamping and no applied\nL123@P1: forces:\nL124@P1: x˙ =\nL125@P1: 1\nL126@P1: γ\nL127@P1: ξ\nL128@P1: (F )\nL129@P1: (t) ≡ ξ\nL130@P1: (v)\nL131@P1: (t), (1)\nL132@P1: where ξ\nL133@P1: (F )\nL134@P1: (t) is the fluctuating thermal force and ξ\nL135@P1: (v)\nL136@P1: (t)\nL137@P1: gives the corresponding velocity fluctuations. From the\nL138@P1: td = ts ts\nL139@P1: position xn-1 xn xn+1\nL140@P1: tc\nL141@P1: xn-2\nL142@P1: ξn\nL143@P1: ξn\nL144@P1: xn+1\nL145@P1: force Fn-2 Fn-1 Fn\nL146@P1: observations\nL147@P1: thermal\nL148@P1: noise\nL149@P1: xn-1 xn\nL150@P1: tn-2 tn-1 tn tn+1\nL151@P1: time ts\nL152@P1: Thursday, October 25, 12\nL153@P1: FIG. 1. Timing diagram for feedback trap with instantaneous\nL154@P1: position measurements. The feedback update interval is ts,\nL155@P1: and there is a delay td = ts between the measurement time\nL156@P1: and the output of the next feedback update. The true position\nL157@P1: of particles xn are indicated at bottom, together with the\nL158@P1: associated thermal noise ξn and force Fn. The force Fn is\nL159@P1: computed from the observed position x¯n available at the time\nL160@P1: Fn is started, nts. At top are indicated the obse